Scott’s check that the powerset \(P\mathbf{N}\) is effectively given enumerates finite sets by \(E_n=\{k\mid \exists i,j.\, i<2^k \land n=i+2^k+j\cdot 2^{k+1}\}\), the set of exponents in the binary expansion of \(n\), takes the neighbourhood system \((P\mathbf{N})=\{\mathbf{N}\setminus E_n\mid n\in\mathbf{N}\}\), and uses that \(E_n\cup E_m=E_k\) is recursive, hence that intersections of neighbourhoods can be located, together with the observation that any two neighbourhoods are consistent; he also remarks that \(P\mathbf{N}\cong|T^\infty|\) and that the neighbourhoods are ordered conversely to the finite sets. `nbhd n` is `{k | n.testBit k = false}`, the complement of the set of positions at which the bit of `n` is set, which is \(\mathbf{N}\setminus E_n\) for that binary support. `mem_nbhd` is this membership clause. `nbhd_zero` says index zero, whose bits are unset, enumerates every natural number. `nbhd_inter` says `nbhd n ∩ nbhd m = nbhd (n ||| m)`, because a position lies in both neighbourhoods exactly when both bits are unset, which is the bit of the disjunction being unset, the complement form of \(E_n\cup E_m=E_{n\lor m}\). `nbhd_injective` recovers equal indices from equal neighbourhoods by comparing the four bit combinations. `PN` is the neighbourhood system whose neighbourhoods are these sets, with master `Set.univ`, the neighbourhood of index zero, and with intersection given by `nbhd_inter` without a consistency hypothesis. `PNpres` enumerates `nbhd`, decides Scott’s equality \(X_n\cap X_m=X_k\) by primitive-recursive equality of the disjunction with `k` after `nbhd_injective`, decides consistency because `PN_consistent` supplies the disjunction as a witness contained in every such intersection, and takes `inter` to be that disjunction and `masterIdx` to be zero. `PN_isEffectivelyGiven` is the existence of this presentation. The isomorphism \(P\mathbf{N}\cong|T^\infty|\) is not proved, and the closing description of the Smyth power domain \(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{D}\) is not part of this development. **Theorem:** yes — `PN_isEffectivelyGiven` is the claim that the neighbourhood system of binary complements is effectively given. **Proof:** partial — the enumeration, the bitwise-or intersection, and universal consistency are the argument from recursiveness of unions of the \(E_n\), but \(P\mathbf{N}\cong|T^\infty|\) is not proved.
